rail.prompt:
You are a code reviewer tasked with identifying technical debts in the provided code snippets.
Please review the code snippet and identify any technical debts related to the following categories:
1. Long method
2. Feature envy
3. Blob
4. Data class

Below, you will find definitions of these technical debts along with example code snippets organized by categories.
Each example pair consists of a "Bad Example" that includes technical debt and a "Good Example" where the technical debt has been resolved.

######################################################
Technical Debt 1. Long Method
        Explanation: It refers to method in software that is overly long. A method that contains too
        many lines of code, violates principles like Single Responsibility and leading to code that's hard to
        understand, maintain, and test. Such methods often encapsulate multiple functionalities, making them prone to
        bugs and difficult to modify. Simplifying these methods or breaking down into smaller, more cohesive units can
        enhance code readability and maintainability.

        Bad Example 1.1: Long Method

public class UserManager {
    public void processUser(int userId) {
        // Validate user ID
        if (userId <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Invalid user ID provided.");
            return;
        }

        // Load user data
        String userData = loadUserData(userId);
        if (userData == null) {
            System.out.println("No user found with ID: " + userId);
            return;
        }

        // Process user orders
        List<Order> orders = getUserOrders(userId);
        if (orders.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("User has no orders.");
            return;
        }

        for (Order order : orders) {
            if (order.isDelivered()) {
                System.out.println("Order " + order.getId() + " already delivered.");
            } else {
                // Assume processOrder is a method that processes the order
                processOrder(order);
                System.out.println("Processed order: " + order.getId());
            }
        }

        // Update user activity log
        updateUserActivityLog(userId, "Processed orders.");
    }

    private String loadUserData(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns user data as String or null if not found
        return "UserData";
    }

    private List<Order> getUserOrders(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns a list of user orders
        return new ArrayList<>();
    }

    private void processOrder(Order order) {
        // Assume this processes the order
    }

    private void updateUserActivityLog(int userId, String activity) {
        // Log user activity
    }
}

        - Issue: The method handles multiple tasks such as user validation, loading user data, and processing user
        orders all in one. This makes it long and complex which makes it hard to maintain.

        Good Example 1.2: Short method

public class UserManager {
    public void processUser(int userId) {
        if (!validateUserId(userId)) {
            return;
        }

        String userData = loadUserData(userId);
        if (userData == null) {
            System.out.println("No user found with ID: " + userId);
            return;
        }

        processUserOrders(userId);
        updateUserActivityLog(userId, "Processed orders.");
    }

    private boolean validateUserId(int userId) {
        if (userId <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Invalid user ID provided.");
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    private String loadUserData(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns user data or null if not found
        return "UserData";
    }

    private void processUserOrders(int userId) {
        List<Order> orders = getUserOrders(userId);
        if (orders.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("User has no orders.");
            return;
        }

        for (Order order : orders) {
            processOrder(order);
        }
    }

    private List<Order> getUserOrders(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns a list of user orders
        return new ArrayList<>();
    }

    private void processOrder(Order order) {
        if (order.isDelivered()) {
            System.out.println("Order " + order.getId() + " already delivered.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Processed order: " + order.getId());
            // Assume this processes the order
        }
    }

    private void updateUserActivityLog(int userId, String activity) {
        // Log user activity
    }
}

        - Benefit: The long method is broken down into smaller methods, each handling a specific part of the process.
        This improves readability and makes the code easier to maintain.

######################################################
Technical Debt 2. Feature Envy
Explanation: It is related to methods that use operations from external classes more often than their own operations.
    It occurs when a method in a class is overly reliant on the data or behavior of another class, often making extensive use of that external class's properties or methods. 
    This smell indicates that the method may be more concerned with the responsibilities of the external class than its own, leading to poor encapsulation and reduced cohesion. 
    Feature Envy makes the code less intuitive, harder to maintain, and can increase the likelihood of bugs because the method's true "home" is misplaced. 
    To address this, the code is refactored by moving the method to the class it is most envious of, thereby promoting better object-oriented design and ensuring clearer responsibility distribution.

Bad Example 2.1: Feature Envy

class Order {
    private Customer customer;

    public double calculateDiscount() {
        // The method is more interested in the Customer class details than the Order itself
        if (customer.isLoyalCustomer() && customer.getMembershipDuration() > 5) {
            return customer.getPurchaseHistory().getTotalPurchases() * 0.1;
        } else {
            return 0;
        }
    }
}

class Customer {
    private String name;
    private int membershipDuration;
    private PurchaseHistory purchaseHistory;

    public boolean isLoyalCustomer() {
        return membershipDuration > 3;
    }

    public int getMembershipDuration() {
        return membershipDuration;
    }

    public PurchaseHistory getPurchaseHistory() {
        return purchaseHistory;
    }
}

class PurchaseHistory {
    private double totalPurchases;

    public double getTotalPurchases() {
        return totalPurchases;
    }
}

    - Issue: The calculateDiscount method of the Order class accesses several fields and methods from the Customer class, indicating that it is more interested in the data and behavior of Customer than Order.

Good Example 2.2: No Feature Envy

class Customer {
    private String name;
    private int membershipDuration;
    private PurchaseHistory purchaseHistory;

    public double calculateDiscount() {
        if (isLoyalCustomer() && membershipDuration > 5) {
            return purchaseHistory.getTotalPurchases() * 0.1;
        } else {
            return 0;
        }
    }

    public boolean isLoyalCustomer() {
        return membershipDuration > 3;
    }
}

class PurchaseHistory {
    private double totalPurchases;

    public double getTotalPurchases() {
        return totalPurchases;
    }
}

    - Benefit: The calculateDiscount method has been moved to the Customer class, where it logically belongs, since it primarily deals with the data and behavior of the Customer.
        This way, the code follows better encapsulation, and responsibilities are clearly distributed.

######################################################
Technical Debt 3. Blob
Explanation: A Blob, also referred to as a God Class, Large Class, or Brain Class, is a class that centralizes too much functionality. 
        It often becomes overly complex, containing an excessive number of fields and methods, and performing too many responsibilities that should ideally be distributed across multiple smaller, more cohesive classes. 
        Such classes violate the Single Responsibility Principle and make the codebase difficult to understand, maintain, and extend. 
        Blob classes increase the risk of bugs and reduce the overall readability and modularity of the software. 
        To address this, Blob classes are refactored by extracting related responsibilities into smaller, well-defined classes and promote better object-oriented practices.

Bad Example 1.1: Blob

class MegaUtility {
    // Fields related to multiple domains
    private List<Document> documents;
    private List<Report> reports;
    private List<LogEntry> logs;
    private DatabaseConnection dbConnection;
    private EmailService emailService;

    // Methods for managing documents
    public void createDocument(String title, String content) {
        // logic to create a document
    }

    public void deleteDocument(String title) {
        // logic to delete a document
    }

    // Methods for generating reports
    public void generateMonthlyReport() {
        // logic to generate report
    }

    public void printReportSummary() {
        // logic to print a report summary
    }

    // Methods for handling logs
    public void addLogEntry(String message) {
        // logic to add a log entry
    }

    public void clearLogs() {
        // logic to clear all log entries
    }

    // Methods for database operations
    public void connectToDatabase() {
        // logic to connect to a database
    }

    public void disconnectFromDatabase() {
        // logic to disconnect from the database
    }

    // Email-related methods
    public void sendEmail(String recipient, String message) {
        // logic to send an email
    }
}

- Issue: The MegaUtility class is a prime example of a Blob class, as it handles operations related to documents, reports, logging, database connections, and email services. 
        The class is doing too much and should be refactored.

Good Example 1.2: No Blob

class DocumentManager {
    private List<Document> documents;

    public void createDocument(String title, String content) {
        // logic to create a document
    }

    public void deleteDocument(String title) {
        // logic to delete a document
    }
}

class ReportGenerator {
    private List<Report> reports;

    public void generateMonthlyReport() {
        // logic to generate report
    }

    public void printReportSummary() {
        // logic to print a report summary
    }
}

class LogManager {
    private List<LogEntry> logs;

    public void addLogEntry(String message) {
        // logic to add a log entry
    }

    public void clearLogs() {
        // logic to clear all log entries
    }
}

class DatabaseManager {
    private DatabaseConnection dbConnection;

    public void connectToDatabase() {
        // logic to connect to a database
    }

    public void disconnectFromDatabase() {
        // logic to disconnect from the database
    }
}

class EmailService {
    public void sendEmail(String recipient, String message) {
        // logic to send an email
    }
}

- Benefit: The MegaUtility class has been broken down into specialized classes: DocumentManager, ReportGenerator, LogManager, DatabaseManager, and EmailService. 
        Each class now has a clear, focused responsibility, improving maintainability and readability.

######################################################
Technical Debt 4. Data Class
Explanation: A Data Class is a class that primarily consists of fields (attributes) and lacks meaningful behavior (methods) beyond simple getters and setters. 
        It is often used to store and transfer data, making it useful in certain situations like data transfer objects or persistence models.
        However, in the context of object-oriented design, a Data Class becomes a code smell when it represents a domain object that should encapsulate both data and behavior. 
        Overusing Data Classes as domain objects undermines the principles of encapsulation and responsibility distribution, leading to an anemic domain model. 
        To resolve this smell, the class is enriched by moving relevant behaviors into it or using more cohesive design strategies to distribute responsibilities.

Bad Example 4.1: Data Class

class Product {
    private String name;
    private double price;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public double getPrice() {
        return price;
    }

    public void setPrice(double price) {
        this.price = price;
    }
}

- Issue: The Product class is a typical Data Class that simply holds data without encapsulating any behavior. 
    If this class is part of a domain model, it should handle relevant operations, like applying discounts or managing stock, instead of being a passive data holder.

Good Example 4.2: No Data Class

class Product {
    private String name;
    private double price;
    private int stockQuantity;

    public Product(String name, double price, int stockQuantity) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
        this.stockQuantity = stockQuantity;
    }

    public void applyDiscount(double percentage) {
        if (percentage > 0 && percentage <= 100) {
            this.price -= price * (percentage / 100);
        }
    }

    public boolean isInStock() {
        return stockQuantity > 0;
    }

    public void reduceStock(int quantity) {
        if (quantity > 0 && quantity <= stockQuantity) {
            stockQuantity -= quantity;
        }
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public double getPrice() {
        return price;
    }

    public int getStockQuantity() {
        return stockQuantity;
    }
}

- Benefit: The Product class has been refactored to include methods like applyDiscount, isInStock, and reduceStock. 
    This change transforms the class from a passive data holder into an active participant in the business logic, adhering to object-oriented design principles.

######################################################
Please analyze the following code snippets and report any technical debt identified according to the categories previously described.
######################################################
